issue_context.body:
If you come from the world of Service Worker and acts a bit like a man in the middle by inspecting every network request that goes in and out  or you are working with the fetch api then you will see that the spec specifies that every Request has an associated AbortSignal.

```js
// you can construct one on your own
new window.Request('/').signal instanceof AbortSignal // yields true

// FYI, this is not the same signal that have been passed in as an argument
var { signal } = new AbortController()
new window.Request('/', { signal }).signal === signal // yields false
```

Now what can this be used for? Well, getting things from the database or convert some file as long as the user is connected
I think we should make the request handler a tiny bit more like the service worker by adding in a abortSignal into every request handler.
I wish i could do something like: 
```js
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  mongodb.getUser(userId, { signal: req.signal })
})

// Same service worker equivalent 
onfetch = evt => {
  evt.respondWith(fetch(getUserRequest, { signal: evt.request.signal }))
})
```
if a client abort or disconnect then so should the call for retrieving something from the database

comments:
There is already an effort to add `AbortController`/`AbortSignal` support throughout the Node.js API where it makes sense. I'm not sure if associating an `AbortSignal` automatically with all http requests is a good idea yet or not -- specifically, it may not make sense at that level. 

You can accomplish what you're looking for with 

```js
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const ac = new AbortController();
  const signal = ac.signal;
  mongodb.getUser(userId, { signal })

  // Sometime after, ac.abort() is called
})
```

@jasnell from [here](https://fetch.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-request-signal), I think that what he's looking for is something somewhat equivalent to the following:

```js
http.createServer((req, res) => {
  const ac = new AbortController();
  req.on('aborted', ()=>ac.abort());
  const signal = ac.signal;
  mongodb.getUser(userId, { signal })
})
```
